<p>
  The first choice when buying a handheld tachometer is how it reads the shaft: by touching it, or by looking at it. Both give an RPM reading, and with a good quartz time base both can be very accurate. The right one depends on what you measure, where you measure it and what else you want to know. This guide sets out the difference and compares the three Systems Tech handheld models that CIE Instruments supplies.
</p>

<h2 id="contact">Contact Tachometers</h2>

<p>
  A contact tachometer has a rubber tip that you press against the end of a rotating shaft. The tip spins with the shaft and the instrument counts the turns. Its strengths are simplicity and one extra capability: with a wheel (peripheral) tip it can read <strong>surface speed</strong> in metres per minute, which is what you need for conveyors, rollers, belts and winders. Its limits are access and speed. You need a safe way to touch the shaft end, and contact tips are limited to a lower top speed because of friction and tip wear.
</p>

<h2 id="photo">Non-Contact (Photo) Tachometers</h2>

<p>
  A photo tachometer fires a beam at a strip of reflective tape on the rotating part and counts the reflections. Nothing touches the machine, so it is safer on exposed shafts, works on small or delicate parts and reaches a much higher speed. It needs tape on the part and a clear line of sight, and it will not read surface speed on its own.
</p>

