<p>
  All three share a 5-digit red LED display, a quartz crystal time base with 0.05% + 1 digit accuracy, automatic last, maximum and minimum memory, four AA cells with auto shut-off, and a padded carrying case.
</p>

<h2 id="choose">How to Choose</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Choose the HTM 590</strong> if you mostly check motors, fans and pumps with an accessible shaft end, or you need surface speed for belts and rollers, and you do not need above 50,000 RPM.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the HTM 560</strong> if the shaft is guarded, small, very fast or awkward to touch, and RPM is all you need.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the HTM 890</strong> if one person does varied work, such as a service engineer who meets both exposed shafts and fast, guarded ones, and wants a single tool for contact RPM, surface speed and non-contact RPM.</li>
</ul>

<Callout type="info" title="Rule of thumb">
  If you can only buy one instrument and your jobs vary, the combined HTM 890 avoids owning two tachometers. If your work is one repeated task, the single-method model is lighter on the budget and on the bag.
</Callout>

